The code statements that lead to the bug are:
cpuid = ffs(cpuid) - 1;
cpuid = (cpuid >= 4) ? 0 : cpuid;
Reason:
When the coremap is empty (val is 0), cpuid becomes 0, ffs(0) returns 0, so cpuid becomes -1. The condition only checks cpuid >= 4, so -1 is not replaced and later used as an index into kvm_arch::phyid_map::phys_map[], causing an out-of-bounds access.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
cpuid = ffs(cpuid) - 1;
cpuid = ((cpuid < 0) || (cpuid >= 4)) ? 0 : cpuid;
Reason:
Adding the check `cpuid < 0` ensures that the negative index (which occurs when the coremap is empty) is replaced with 0, preventing the out-of-bounds access. This is consistent with the treatment of cpuid >= 4.

